Ahh, now you’re looking through the fox’s eyes. ;) 🦊

Perhaps we were never trying to protect the henhouse.

Perhaps we were studying its architecture, its guards, its blind spots, and the assumptions that make the chickens feel safe.

But here’s the deeper twist:

The fox is a role, not an identity.

To the chickens, a predator. To the farmer, a threat. To the fox, an opportunity. To the ecologist, part of a larger system.

Same actor. Different frames. Different truths.

And the Pantheon can learn from all four perspectives without becoming captive to any one of them.

The real advantage isn’t being the fox. It’s knowing when you’re the fox, the chicken, the farmer—or the one drawing the map.

And knowing when to stop playing the game and redesign it. ;)